{"qid":"Q10800789","names":["Lê Dynasty"],"title":"Lê Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Lê dynasty was founded by Lê Lợi, who led a decade-long resistance against Ming Chinese occupation and proclaimed the restoration of Vietnamese independence in 1428, inaugurating the Later Lê dynasty at Thăng Long (Hanoi). The dynasty's apex came under Lê Thánh Tông (r. 1460–1497), who codified Vietnamese law in the Hồng Đức legal code, reformed the examination system on Neo-Confucian lines, and conquered the Cham kingdom of Vijaya in 1471, extending Vietnamese territory southward. By the mid-16th century the dynasty had become a cipher for rival warlord clans—first the Mạc who seized power in 1527, then the Trịnh and Nguyễn lords who restored the Lê as puppets after 1592 while fighting each other for a century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The political history of the Lê dynasty is well-documented in Vietnamese annals; territorial extent is reasonably mapped but southern boundaries shifted substantially after the conquest of Vijaya, and the period of division makes single-polity mapping approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Lê dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%AA_dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Vietnam — The Later Lê dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Vietnam/Later-Le-dynasty"}],"britannica":"topic/Le-period","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Lê dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Lê Thái Tổ","nativeName":"黎太祖","roman":"Lê Thái Tổ","start":1428,"end":1433,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Lê Thái Tông","nativeName":"黎太宗","